From cb24fc0b765df47312f72125ee2fff8db3e47a84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mitar <mitar.git@tnode.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:37:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Bug 2090791 - [Linux] Launch local handler applications
 through GIO r=stransky,gtk-reviewers

An executable chosen as the handler of a MIME type or URI scheme, or named
by a mailcap file, was run with nsIProcess, while applications from
desktop entries are launched through GIO. Such a handler received no
activation token on Wayland, inherited MOZ_APP_LAUNCHER, which the GIO
launch context unsets for third-party handlers (bug 1494436), and stayed
in Firefox's cgroup, where GLib cannot give it a systemd scope of its own.

nsIGIOService::CreateAppFromExecutable() describes the executable as a
desktop entry whose Exec key passes the URI unchanged (%u with
X-GIO-NoFuse) or the path of the local file (%f). The path is quoted,
and % is doubled. We fall back to nsIProcess when GLib refuses the
executable: when it is no executable file, so that the error is still
reported, and when its path contains %, as GLib checks the Exec
key before it turns %% back into %. OpenBSD keeps nsIProcess.

On Wayland the launch waits for the activation token, as for other GIO
handlers, so errors after the executable check are logged instead of
returned, and GLib spawns the handler through gio-launch-desktop, so it is
no longer a direct child of Firefox.

Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D340772

Origin: https://github.com/mozilla-firefox/firefox/commit/cb24fc0b765df47312f72125ee2fff8db3e47a84
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-11-23
